4. 📈 PDP, ICE and ALE
Partial Dependence Plot (PDP)
Shows the average prediction as one feature varies while all others keep their observed values.
ICE (Individual Conditional Expectation)
One line per sample. If the lines disagree, the PDP average is hiding an interaction.
from sklearn.inspection import PartialDependenceDisplay

PartialDependenceDisplay.from_estimator(
    model, X_val, features=["income", "age"],
    kind="both",            # "average" = PDP, "individual" = ICE, "both" = overlay
    subsample=200, grid_resolution=30)
ALE (Accumulated Local Effects)
Like PDP but robust to correlated features, because it only uses realistic nearby values. Libraries: alibi, PyALE.
WARNING: PDP assumes features are independent. With correlated features it evaluates impossible combinations (e.g. age 5 with 20 years of experience). Prefer ALE then.
The PDP also connects to Day 10: it lets you check that a monotonic constraint behaves as intended.
5. 🎲 SHAP Deep Dive
The idea
Shapley values come from game theory. Each feature is a "player", the prediction is the "payout", and a feature's SHAP value is its average marginal contribution across all feature orderings.
prediction = base_value + sum(SHAP values of all features)
This is additivity: the contributions always sum exactly to the gap between the prediction and the average prediction.
