Units matter
• TreeExplainer on a classifier explains the log-odds (margin), not the probability.
• Positive SHAP raises the log-odds of the positive class, negative lowers it.
• Calibration (Day 10) changes the probability output, but SHAP on the raw model explains the uncalibrated margin. Mention this when reporting.
Plots
import shap

explainer   = shap.TreeExplainer(final_xgb)
shap_values = explainer(X_test_transformed)       # needs transformed data

shap.plots.bar(shap_values)               # global: mean |SHAP|
shap.plots.beeswarm(shap_values)          # global: size + direction
shap.plots.waterfall(shap_values[0])      # local: one prediction
shap.plots.scatter(shap_values[:, "income"],
                   color=shap_values)     # dependence + auto interaction
Reading a beeswarm
• Vertical order: importance (top is most important)
• X position: impact on the prediction (right pushes up)
• Colour: feature value (red high, blue low)
• Red dots on the right means a high value pushes the prediction up
SHAP interaction values
inter = explainer.shap_interaction_values(X_sample)   # shape (n, f, f)
Separates each feature's main effect from pairwise interaction effects. Costly: use a sample.
IMPORTANT: SHAP explains model behaviour, not causality. And with correlated features, credit is split between them arbitrarily, so rankings can be unstable.
6. 🍋 LIME
LIME explains one prediction by:
1. Perturbing the sample to create nearby fake samples
2. Getting the black-box predictions for them
3. Fitting a small weighted linear model around the point
4. Reading that model's coefficients as the explanation
from lime.lime_tabular import LimeTabularExplainer

exp = LimeTabularExplainer(X_train_arr, feature_names=cols,
                           class_names=["no", "yes"], mode="classification")

out = exp.explain_instance(X_test_arr[0], model.predict_proba, num_features=8)
out.show_in_notebook()

