8. 🌍 Real-World Issues
Explaining a pipeline
SHAP needs the transformed features the model actually saw.
prep   = pipe.named_steps["prep"]
model  = pipe.named_steps["model"]
X_t    = prep.transform(X_test)
names  = prep.get_feature_names_out()

sv = shap.TreeExplainer(model)(X_t)
sv.feature_names = list(names)
NOTE: One-hot encoding splits a feature into many columns. Sum SHAP values across a feature's dummy columns to get its total effect.
Imbalance and SMOTE
SMOTE only runs at fit time, so explain on real (non-resampled) data. Explanations of a model trained with class weights reflect the shifted decision boundary, so say so.
Correlated features
Group them, drop one, or use ALE and clustered SHAP. Never over-interpret a single ranking.
Large data
Explain a sample (500 to 5,000 rows). Use shap.sample(X, n) or shap.kmeans for background data.
Reporting to stakeholders
• Lead with the top 3 to 5 drivers in plain language
• Show one or two local examples (waterfall)
• State limits: correlation, not causation
